// SWRV Kingdom Bible — Strong's Sense Map
// Deep contextual definitions for Hebrew and Greek words keyed by Strong's ID.
// Each entry: primarySense (authoritative gloss), etymology (word origin),
// senses[] (usage range), contextNote (theological/contextual insight),
// notMeantWhen (disambiguation from false cognates / shallow glosses).
window.STRONGS_SENSE_MAP = {

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  HEBREW — Divine Names & Titles
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

"H430": {
  primarySense: "Elohim — the majestic plural for the one God of Israel; the Creator, Ruler, and Judge of all things.",
  etymology: "Plural of El (H410) or Eloah (H433); the -im ending is a Hebrew masculine plural. When used of Israel's God, it takes singular verbs (bara', 'he created'), marking it as a plural of majesty, not quantity.",
  senses: [
    "The God of Israel — Creator, Lawgiver, Redeemer; the one who spoke all things into being (Gen 1:1).",
    "Divine beings or the heavenly court — 'sons of Elohim' (Job 1:6), messengers in God's presence.",
    "Pagan gods — idols or foreign deities referred to with this word (Exod 20:3), always contrasted with Israel's God.",
    "Human judges or rulers — in Ps 82:6 God addresses human judges as 'elohim' because they exercise delegated divine authority.",
    "Superlative force — occasionally adds intensity: 'a mighty wind' (ruach Elohim, Gen 1:2)."
  ],
  contextNote: "Genesis 1:1 opens with 'In the beginning Elohim created' — the plural name with a singular verb establishes from page one that Israel's God is uniquely one yet mysteriously full. The name Elohim emphasizes power, transcendence, and cosmic authority. When the narrative shifts to covenant relationship it typically shifts to YHWH (H3068) or the compound YHWH Elohim.",
  notMeantWhen: "Elohim does NOT imply multiple gods. The singular verb construction rules that out. It is not equivalent to 'a god' or 'divine force' — its canonical use is the personal Creator of Genesis 1."
},

"H3068": {
  primarySense: "YHWH — the personal covenant name of Israel's God, usually rendered LORD in English. Derived from the verb 'to be': the self-existent, eternally present One.",
  etymology: "From hayah (H1961, 'to be, to exist, to happen'). God revealed this name to Moses at the burning bush: 'I AM WHO I AM' (Exod 3:14). The Tetragrammaton YHWH was considered so sacred that Jewish tradition substituted Adonai when reading. Modern English translators follow this by writing LORD in small capitals.",
  senses: [
    "The self-existent One — the God who simply IS, not contingent on anything outside himself.",
    "The covenant God — the name used specifically in relationship with Israel, marking promises, laws, and redemption.",
    "The God who acts in history — YHWH is the one who brought Israel out of Egypt (Exod 20:2), who speaks to prophets, who judges nations.",
    "The God of steadfast love — YHWH is consistently paired with hesed (H2617), faithfulness, and compassion.",
    "The God who is present — 'I AM' is not past tense; it signals perpetual, active presence."
  ],
  contextNote: "Every occurrence of LORD (small caps) in English OT translations represents YHWH. The name carries immense weight: to call on the name of YHWH is to enter covenant relationship (Joel 2:32). In Exodus 6 God distinguishes: 'I appeared to Abraham as El Shaddai, but by my name YHWH I was not known to them' — the name itself signals a new depth of revelation.",
  notMeantWhen: "YHWH is not a generic word for 'god' or 'deity.' It is the personal name of Israel's God. When the text says 'the LORD your God' it is simultaneously using the name (YHWH) and the relational title (Elohim = your God)."
},

"H136": {
  primarySense: "Adonai — 'my Lord' or 'the Lord'; a title of supreme authority and ownership. Spoken aloud wherever YHWH appeared in the text, out of reverence.",
  etymology: "From adon (H113, 'lord, master, owner') with the possessive suffix -ai ('my'). Used of both human masters and God; when referring to God it became a title of absolute sovereignty.",
  senses: [
    "God as absolute sovereign — Adonai affirms that God is not merely one lord among many but the Lord over all.",
    "Substitute for YHWH in Jewish liturgical reading — wherever YHWH appears, Adonai is spoken.",
    "Address of reverence and submission — 'O Lord (Adonai), my God' expresses total dependence.",
    "Used by the prophets in visions — 'I saw the Lord (Adonai) seated on a throne' (Isa 6:1)."
  ],
  contextNote: "When Isaiah sees the throne-room vision (Isa 6:1) the word is Adonai — Lord of all. When prophets begin 'Thus says the Lord GOD' the Hebrew is often 'Thus says Adonai YHWH,' stacking the title and name to say: the Master who is the self-existent covenant God has spoken.",
  notMeantWhen: "Adonai when applied to humans simply means 'sir' or 'master.' Context always determines whether divine or human lordship is meant. The capital-L 'Lord' in English usually signals the divine use."
},

"H410": {
  primarySense: "El — the short, ancient Semitic name for God emphasizing power, might, and divine strength.",
  etymology: "The root el or ʾl appears across Semitic languages (Ugaritic, Akkadian, Phoenician) for the chief deity. In Hebrew it carries the basic meaning of 'strength' or 'power.' Often used in compound divine names: El Shaddai (God Almighty), El Elyon (God Most High), El Roi (God Who Sees).",
  senses: [
    "God in his raw power and might — El is the strong one who can act, protect, and deliver.",
    "God Most High (El Elyon) — the God who rules above all other so-called gods and all earthly powers (Gen 14:18-20).",
    "God Almighty (El Shaddai) — the God of the patriarchs, associated with blessing, fertility, and covenant abundance (Gen 17:1).",
    "The God who sees (El Roi) — Hagar's name for God after he appeared to her in the wilderness (Gen 16:13).",
    "Used poetically as a synonym for Elohim or YHWH in psalms and poetry."
  ],
  contextNote: "El is the backbone of Hebrew divine vocabulary. It shows up in personal names (Elijah = 'My God is YHWH', Michael = 'Who is like God?') and in compounds that reveal divine character. Each El-compound name taught Israel a new facet of who God is in a specific moment of their story.",
  notMeantWhen: "El alone does not carry the covenant specificity of YHWH or the full majesty-plural weight of Elohim. It is the raw claim: there is a God, and he is powerful."
},

"H7307": {
  primarySense: "Ruach — wind, breath, or spirit; the invisible, moving, life-giving force of God or of living creatures. Context always determines which meaning applies.",
  etymology: "Related to Aramaic ruach and Arabic ruh. The basic sense is moving air — from a breeze to a violent storm. By extension it becomes breath (the sign of life) and then spirit (the inner animating principle). The same word covers God's Spirit and the human spirit.",
  senses: [
    "Physical wind — ruach as natural phenomenon: 'a ruach from the LORD swept across the water' (Num 11:31).",
    "Breath — the breath of living creatures; 'all in whose nostrils was the breath of the spirit of life' (Gen 7:22).",
    "The animating spirit of a person — inner disposition, will, attitude: 'a crushed ruach' (Ps 34:18).",
    "The Spirit of God — the divine ruach that hovered over creation (Gen 1:2), filled craftsmen with skill (Exod 31:3), empowered judges and kings, and is poured out in the new covenant.",
    "Divine empowerment — the ruach of YHWH coming upon someone to enable prophecy, leadership, or miraculous action."
  ],
  contextNote: "Genesis 1:2 says ruach Elohim hovered (or moved, swept) over the face of the waters. This is the Spirit of God present at creation — active, dynamic, brooding over chaos before order comes. The same word that later fills Bezalel for artistic skill (Exod 31:3) and moves Isaiah (Isa 61:1) and is poured on all flesh in Joel 2:28. The continuity of the word across all these uses is intentional.",
  notMeantWhen: "Ruach never means a ghost in the horror sense, an ethereal soul floating after death, or an impersonal force. It is always personal: God's breath, a person's spirit, the wind that God sends. Context makes the specific sense clear."
},

"H5315": {
  primarySense: "Nephesh — the living self; the whole person as a breathing, desiring, needing being. Often translated 'soul' but it does not mean an immaterial part that separates from the body.",
  etymology: "Related to a root meaning 'throat' or 'neck' — the passage of breath. A nephesh is a creature that breathes. In Genesis 2:7, Adam became a 'living nephesh' when God breathed into him — not that he received a soul added to a body, but that the whole being became alive.",
  senses: [
    "A living creature — any breathing being, human or animal (Gen 1:20, 1:21, 1:24 all use nephesh for animals).",
    "The whole person — 'my nephesh' often means simply 'I' or 'me': 'Bless the LORD, O my soul' = 'I bless the LORD with everything I am.'",
    "Desire, appetite, longing — the throat that craves: 'My nephesh longs for you' (Ps 42:1, literally: 'My throat pants').",
    "Life itself — 'he sought my nephesh' means 'he sought to kill me'; 'lay down his nephesh' means 'lay down his life.'",
    "An individual person — counted in census lists: 'seventy nepheshoth went down into Egypt' (Gen 46:27)."
  ],
  contextNote: "The Hebrew view of persons is holistic, not dualistic. You don't have a nephesh; you ARE a nephesh. This reshapes how we read 'do not fear those who kill the body but cannot kill the soul' (Matt 10:28) — the Greek psyche (soul) there carries similar holistic weight. Physical death does not mean the nephesh is liberated; rather the nephesh dies and resurrection reunites the whole person.",
  notMeantWhen: "Nephesh does NOT mean an immortal, immaterial soul that naturally survives death independently of the body. That concept comes from Greek philosophy (especially Plato), not Hebrew Scripture. The nephesh can die (Num 23:10, Ps 22:29)."
},

"H5397": {
  primarySense: "Neshamah — the breath of life breathed directly by God into Adam; sometimes distinguished from ruach as the most intimate, God-given spark of life.",
  etymology: "Related to nasham, 'to pant, to breathe hard.' While ruach is moving air (wind, spirit broadly), neshamah is specifically the breath in the nostrils — the sharp intake of life.",
  senses: [
    "The divine breath that made Adam a living being (Gen 2:7): 'God breathed into his nostrils the neshamah of life.'",
    "The breath within humans as their life principle — 'the neshamah of the Almighty gives them understanding' (Job 32:8).",
    "Used in judgment contexts: God can withhold or withdraw this breath (Job 34:14).",
    "The human spirit capable of searching deep things (Prov 20:27: 'The neshamah of man is the lamp of the LORD')."
  ],
  contextNote: "In Genesis 2:7, three elements combine: dust + neshamah of life + becoming a living nephesh. The neshamah is specifically the divine act of impartation — God bending down and breathing directly. This is the most intimate of the three Hebrew spirit-words and is used almost exclusively for the human-divine breath relationship.",
  notMeantWhen: "Neshamah is not a synonym for the Holy Spirit nor for the immortal soul. It is the creaturely breath-of-life that God imparts and sustains."
},

"H1320": {
  primarySense: "Basar — flesh; the physical body of a living creature; by extension, all mortal humanity in its creaturely weakness and limitation, without moral evil implied by default.",
  etymology: "The basic meaning is the soft tissue, the meat of a body. Extends to cover the whole body, then all living creatures ('all flesh'), then humankind in contrast to God.",
  senses: [
    "Physical flesh — the body's tissue, meat: 'bone of my bones and flesh of my flesh' (Gen 2:23).",
    "The whole body — 'my flesh rests securely' (Ps 16:9).",
    "A blood relative — 'he is our brother, our own flesh' (Gen 37:27); kinship language.",
    "All living creatures — 'all flesh' (kol basar) means every living being: humans and animals (Gen 6:17).",
    "Humanity in its creatureliness — mortal, fragile, limited: 'flesh and blood cannot inherit the kingdom' (1 Cor 15:50 uses sarx similarly).",
    "In later usage (especially Paul), the flesh as the mode of existence oriented away from God — but this is contextual, not the word's default meaning."
  ],
  contextNote: "Context is everything with basar. In Genesis 2 it is beautiful and good — the stuff of marriage and kinship. In Isaiah 40:6 it signals mortality and transience: 'All flesh is grass.' In prophetic judgment 'all flesh shall see the glory of God' means every living person. When Paul uses sarx (Greek cognate) in Romans 7-8 to mean the sin-prone self, he is extending a metaphor beyond the word's literal range — basar itself is neutral.",
  notMeantWhen: "Basar does not inherently mean sinful human nature. That reading imports Paul's theological extension back into the Hebrew text where it does not belong. 'Flesh' in Genesis simply means the physical, mortal stuff of living creatures."
},

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  HEBREW — Covenant & Relationship
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

"H1285": {
  primarySense: "Berith — covenant; a solemn, binding agreement between parties that creates or redefines a relationship, typically sealed with an oath and a sign.",
  etymology: "Uncertain root — possibly from bara' ('to cut,' since covenants were ratified by cutting sacrificial animals in two), or from an Akkadian cognate meaning 'bond' or 'fetter.' The 'cutting of a covenant' (karat berith) is standard Hebrew idiom.",
  senses: [
    "A covenant between humans — political treaty, marriage bond, or alliance: Abraham and Abimelech (Gen 21:27).",
    "The Noahic covenant — God's unilateral promise to never again destroy all life; sign: the rainbow (Gen 9:8-17).",
    "The Abrahamic covenant — promise of land, offspring, and blessing to all nations; sign: circumcision (Gen 15, 17).",
    "The Mosaic/Sinaitic covenant — conditional covenant of law between YHWH and Israel; sign: Sabbath (Exod 24).",
    "The Davidic covenant — promise of an eternal dynasty through David's line (2 Sam 7).",
    "The New Covenant (Jer 31:31-34) — God's promise to write the law on hearts, forgive sins fully, and know him personally; ratified by Christ's blood (Luke 22:20)."
  ],
  contextNote: "Covenant is the scaffolding of the entire Bible. Every major section of Scripture is organized around a covenant between God and his people. Unlike a contract (which exchanges goods), a covenant creates a relationship — it says 'I will be your God and you will be my people.' The new covenant does not abolish old covenants but fulfills and surpasses them, internalizing what was external.",
  notMeantWhen: "Berith is not a casual promise or general 'deal.' It carries legal, relational, and theological weight. Breaking a covenant was the gravest possible offense in the ancient Near East."
},

"H2617": {
  primarySense: "Hesed — covenant love; loyal, steadfast, unfailing love that goes beyond what is strictly required; sometimes translated lovingkindness, mercy, steadfast love. It is the relational glue of the covenant.",
  etymology: "Root sense is disputed: may relate to 'eagerness' or 'devotion.' But in use, hesed consistently means loyalty that persists through failure — the love of a covenant partner who refuses to give up even when the other party has broken the terms.",
  senses: [
    "God's steadfast love for Israel — the love that endures even Israel's repeated unfaithfulness (Ps 136 repeats 'his hesed endures forever' in every verse).",
    "Loyal love between humans — Ruth's devotion to Naomi is called hesed (Ruth 1:8; 3:10).",
    "The characteristic attribute of YHWH — Exod 34:6 defines God: 'YHWH, YHWH, a God merciful and gracious, slow to anger, abounding in hesed and faithfulness.'",
    "Covenant obligation faithfully performed — helping kin in need, burying the dead honorably, keeping promises against all odds.",
    "The love that defines the new covenant — 'I desire hesed, not sacrifice' (Hos 6:6), quoted by Jesus twice in Matthew."
  ],
  contextNote: "Hesed is arguably the most important attribute word in the Hebrew Bible. It is relational, not abstract. You cannot have hesed by yourself; it is always directed toward another. It combines love with loyalty, grace with obligation. When the NT says 'God is love' (1 John 4:8) it is articulating in Greek what hesed means in Hebrew.",
  notMeantWhen: "Hesed is not merely sentiment or emotion. It is love that acts — specifically, love that acts in faithful, costly ways toward the person to whom one is bound. It is not tolerance, niceness, or permissiveness."
},

"H3722": {
  primarySense: "Kaphar — to cover, to atone, to make propitiation; the central verb of Israel's sacrificial system. It describes the act that removes the barrier between sinful people and a holy God.",
  etymology: "The root may be related to a word meaning 'pitch' or 'covering' (the same root covers Noah's ark with pitch, Gen 6:14). To atone is literally to cover over — the sin is covered from God's sight by the blood of sacrifice.",
  senses: [
    "To make atonement — the technical term for what sacrifices accomplish (Lev 1:4; 4:20, 26, 31, 35).",
    "To cover sin so that God's wrath does not fall — 'the priest shall make atonement for him and he shall be forgiven' (Lev 4:31).",
    "To ransom or redeem — in some contexts kaphar overlaps with paying a price to cancel a debt or penalty (Num 35:31-33).",
    "To propitiate — to turn away divine anger by an appropriate offering.",
    "Used metaphorically of human acts of reconciliation — Jacob's gifts to Esau to 'appease' him use this root (Gen 32:20)."
  ],
  contextNote: "The Day of Atonement (Yom Kippur) gets its name from this verb. On that day, the high priest would make kaphar for himself, for the priesthood, and for all Israel — covering the accumulated sins of the entire year. The New Testament authors (especially Hebrews) argue that animal sacrifices could not truly and finally kaphar sin, but Christ's blood accomplished what the shadows pointed toward.",
  notMeantWhen: "Kaphar in the sacrificial system does not mean sin is forgotten or that it never happened. It means it is covered — the legal consequence is set aside. The blood dealt with the problem in God's legal framework; it did not erase the moral reality."
},

"H4899": {
  primarySense: "Mashiach — anointed one; a person set apart by the ritual pouring of oil as a sign of divine appointment to a specific role: king, priest, or prophet.",
  etymology: "From mashach (H4886), 'to smear or anoint with oil.' In the ancient Near East, anointing with oil installed kings in office and consecrated priests. The Greek translation is Christos (Christ).",
  senses: [
    "The anointed king — Saul, David, and their successors are called YHWH's mashiach; to harm the king is to touch the LORD's anointed (1 Sam 24:6).",
    "The anointed priest — Aaron and his sons were anointed with oil at their ordination (Exod 29:7).",
    "Prophets — occasionally called anointed ('touch not my anointed ones,' Ps 105:15).",
    "The eschatological King — as the Davidic dynasty failed and went into exile, the hope of a coming ultimate mashiach grew. Daniel 9:25-26 uses the term for the coming prince.",
    "Jesus as THE Messiah — the NT's central claim is that Jesus of Nazareth is the fulfillment of every layer of messianic anticipation: the ultimate prophet, priest, and king."
  ],
  contextNote: "When Christians say 'Jesus Christ' they are saying 'Jesus the Anointed One.' The OT office of mashiach — always pointing forward — finds its terminus in Jesus. The early church's proclamation that 'Jesus is Lord and Christ' (Acts 2:36) was simultaneously a political claim (he is king) and a priestly claim (he is the one who makes atonement) and a prophetic claim (he is the Word of God made flesh).",
  notMeantWhen: "Mashiach is not a title that Jesus gave himself constantly; it was a hope-laden OT category that his disciples came to apply to him after his death and resurrection. Not every 'anointed one' in the OT is messianic prophecy — most refer to kings or priests of their own time."
},

"H3444": {
  primarySense: "Yeshua — salvation, deliverance; the act of God rescuing his people from danger, oppression, or the consequences of sin. Also the Hebrew name behind the name 'Jesus.'",
  etymology: "From yasha (H3467), 'to save, deliver, give victory.' The noun describes the concrete outcome of divine saving action. Joshua (Yehoshua) means 'YHWH saves'; Jesus is its Greek form.",
  senses: [
    "Military deliverance — God saving Israel from enemy armies; Ps 3:8 'salvation belongs to YHWH.'",
    "Personal rescue — God delivering an individual from danger, illness, or enemies.",
    "Broader salvation — deliverance from sin and its consequences; the messianic age of total restoration.",
    "YHWH himself as salvation — 'He has become my yeshua' (Exod 15:2); God is not merely the agent but the substance of salvation.",
    "The name Jesus — in Matt 1:21 the angel explains: 'You shall call his name Jesus (Yeshua) for he will save (yasha) his people from their sins.' The name IS the mission."
  ],
  contextNote: "Salvation in the Hebrew Bible is almost never purely spiritual or purely physical — it is both. God saves from enemies AND from sin AND from death. The scope always matters. When Isaiah 52-53 speaks of the Servant whose suffering brings 'yeshua,' it includes healing of the whole person and all nations. The NT expands that same totality.",
  notMeantWhen: "Yeshua does not mean merely personal inner peace or going to heaven when you die. The Hebrew concept of salvation is embodied, communal, and cosmic — the restoration of shalom to all creation."
},

"H3519": {
  primarySense: "Kavod — weight, glory, honor; the palpable, visible manifestation of God's presence and majesty that fills spaces and overwhelms the senses.",
  etymology: "From kaved (H3515), 'to be heavy, weighty.' Glory is literally 'weightiness' — what is substantial, impressive, and commands attention. Human kavod means reputation and honor; divine kavod means the blazing, visible density of God's presence.",
  senses: [
    "Human honor and reputation — a person's kavod is their standing in the community; to shame someone is to strip their kavod.",
    "Material wealth — 'his kavod' can mean his possessions and prosperity (Gen 31:1).",
    "The visible glory of YHWH — the cloud and fire that filled the tabernacle (Exod 40:34-35) and temple (1 Kgs 8:10-11); the blazing presence that Moses glimpsed on Sinai.",
    "The universal declaration of God's glory — 'The heavens declare the kavod of God' (Ps 19:1); creation itself is the overflow of divine weightiness.",
    "The eschatological filling of the earth with God's kavod — Num 14:21; Hab 2:14."
  ],
  contextNote: "When Ezekiel sees the kavod of YHWH departing from the temple (Ezek 10) it is the most devastating moment in the book — the real presence is leaving. When it returns to the new temple (Ezek 43) it is the climax of restoration. The NT applies this kavod language directly to Jesus: John 1:14 'we have seen his glory (doxa)' — the Word made flesh is the kavod tabernacling among us.",
  notMeantWhen: "Kavod is not merely fame, applause, or subjective admiration. The divine kavod is objective — it physically overwhelms; priests cannot stand in it (1 Kgs 8:11). It is real, weighty, and consuming."
},

"H6944": {
  primarySense: "Qodesh — holiness; the state of being set apart for God, separate from the common and the impure. God himself is the only source and standard of holiness.",
  etymology: "The root may relate to 'cut' or 'separate.' Holiness is fundamentally separateness: God is utterly unlike everything else. Things and people become holy by being set apart to him.",
  senses: [
    "God's essential nature — 'Holy, holy, holy is YHWH of hosts' (Isa 6:3); the tripled form is the Hebrew superlative, the ultimate attribute.",
    "Objects consecrated to God — the tabernacle vessels, the temple precincts, the Sabbath day are all qodesh.",
    "Israel as a holy people — set apart from the nations to belong to YHWH (Exod 19:6).",
    "Moral purity — as the people of a holy God, Israel is called to ethical behavior: 'Be holy because I am holy' (Lev 11:44).",
    "The Holy Spirit (ruach ha-qodesh) — the Spirit of God in his separateness and purity."
  ],
  contextNote: "Holiness runs through every book of the Bible as the core attribute of God that everything else derives from. God's love is holy love; his anger is holy anger; his mercy is holy mercy. The problem of the entire Bible is how sinful humans can be in the presence of a holy God — and the answer is atonement, ultimately through Christ, who is qodesh itself made flesh.",
  notMeantWhen: "Holiness is not merely being good or respectable. Its root meaning is otherness, separateness. God is holy before he does anything — he is other by nature. Human holiness is always derivative: we are holy because God sets us apart, not by our own achievement."
},

"H1254": {
  primarySense: "Bara — to create; the specific word used exclusively of God's creative activity with no human subject and no pre-existing material required. Not 'making from' but 'bringing into existence.'",
  etymology: "The root appears only with God as subject in the OT. This is theologically significant: no human or lesser being bara's anything. The word carries the connotation of effortless, authoritative origination.",
  senses: [
    "Creation ex nihilo (from nothing) — Genesis 1:1 uses bara for the heavens and earth; no pre-existing material is mentioned.",
    "Creating new kinds of things — bara is used when something categorically new comes into being: 'God created great sea creatures' (Gen 1:21) and 'God created man in his image' (Gen 1:27).",
    "Eschatological re-creation — 'I will create new heavens and a new earth' (Isa 65:17); the same God who bara'd originally will bara' the renewal of all things.",
    "Transformation that only God can do — 'Create in me a clean heart, O God' (Ps 51:10); only God can do this kind of heart-work."
  ],
  contextNote: "The Bible opens with the statement that sets everything in motion: 'In the beginning, God created.' The word bara signals: this is uniquely divine action. Humans make (asah), form (yatsar), and build (banah); only God bara's. The New Testament connects this to the Son: 'All things were made through him' (John 1:3); 'He is before all things' (Col 1:17).",
  notMeantWhen: "Bara should not be conflated with asah (to make, to fashion from existing material). Genesis 1 uses both words — creation involved both origination and formation, but bara marks the uniquely divine moments."
},

"H2451": {
  primarySense: "Chokmah — wisdom; the practical skill of living well in God's ordered world; knowing how creation actually works and aligning with it. More than intelligence — it is the art of living rightly.",
  etymology: "Related to a root meaning 'to be skilled, experienced, expert.' Wisdom in Hebrew is practical before it is philosophical. The wise craftsman (Exod 31:3) and the wise ruler (1 Kgs 3:12) both have chokmah — they know how things work and how to act accordingly.",
  senses: [
    "Practical skill — Bezalel is filled with chokmah to build the tabernacle (Exod 31:3); skilled artisans are called 'wise of heart.'",
    "Judicial discernment — Solomon's chokmah enables him to judge between competing claims (1 Kgs 3).",
    "Moral wisdom — knowing how to live rightly before God and among people (Prov 1:2-7).",
    "Cosmic wisdom — Wisdom personified as present at creation (Prov 8:22-31); God created through wisdom.",
    "The fear of the LORD as wisdom's foundation — 'The fear of the LORD is the beginning of wisdom' (Prov 9:10); true wisdom starts with right relationship to God.",
    "Christ as wisdom — Paul calls Christ 'the power of God and the wisdom of God' (1 Cor 1:24); Col 2:3 says all treasures of wisdom are hidden in him."
  ],
  contextNote: "Hebrew wisdom is never merely intellectual. The wisdom literature (Proverbs, Job, Ecclesiastes) wrestles with how to live faithfully in a world that is ordered but often confusing. Wisdom requires observation, experience, humility, and above all the fear of YHWH — the recognition that God is God and we are not. Folly (its opposite) is not stupidity but the practical denial of God's ordering of reality.",
  notMeantWhen: "Chokmah is not abstract philosophical reasoning or book learning. The fool in Proverbs may be educated but chooses to live as if there is no God or moral order. Wisdom is always concrete, relational, and embodied."
},

"H3820": {
  primarySense: "Lev — heart; the inner person in their totality: mind, will, emotions, and decision-making center. Hebrew thought locates the whole inner life in the heart, not just feelings.",
  etymology: "The physical organ, but immediately extended to the center of a person's inner life. In Hebrew anthropology there is no separation between thinking and feeling — both happen in the lev. 'Thinking in the heart' is the normal Hebrew idiom for deliberate reasoning.",
  senses: [
    "The seat of thought and planning — 'As a man thinks in his heart, so is he' (Prov 23:7); 'the thoughts of his heart' (Gen 6:5).",
    "The center of decision and will — to 'set your heart' is to decide; to 'harden the heart' is to refuse.",
    "The seat of emotion — joy, grief, fear, love, courage all live in the lev.",
    "The whole inner person — Shema: 'Love the LORD your God with all your heart (lev), soul (nephesh), and strength (meod)' (Deut 6:5) means with every part of your inner and outer being.",
    "The place where sin originates — 'The heart is deceitful above all things and desperately sick' (Jer 17:9).",
    "The target of new covenant transformation — 'I will put my law within them and write it on their hearts' (Jer 31:33); 'I will give you a new heart' (Ezek 36:26)."
  ],
  contextNote: "When the Bible commands us to love God with all our heart it is calling for a total, integrated response — not just emotional feeling but aligned thinking, willing, deciding, and acting. The 'new heart' of Ezekiel 36 is the same thing as the born-again nature of John 3: a fundamental reorientation of the whole inner person toward God.",
  notMeantWhen: "The Hebrew lev is not merely the seat of emotions (feelings). It is primarily the cognitive-volitional center. English 'follow your heart' suggests feeling; Hebrew 'guard your heart' (Prov 4:23) means guard the whole center of your inner life, especially your thoughts and decisions."
},

"H1961": {
  primarySense: "Hayah — to be, to exist, to happen, to become; the most fundamental verb of existence in Hebrew. Source of the divine name YHWH.",
  etymology: "The basic copula and existential verb. In Exodus 3:14, God says ehyeh asher ehyeh ('I AM WHO I AM' or 'I WILL BE WHAT I WILL BE'). The name YHWH is the third-person form of this verb: 'He IS' or 'He CAUSES TO BE.'",
  senses: [
    "Simple existence — 'In the beginning was (hayah) the word' equivalent in Hebrew idiom.",
    "Becoming — 'Let there be light' (yehi or) = 'let light come into being/become.'",
    "Divine self-identification — 'I AM WHO I AM' (Exod 3:14) defines God as the source of all being, contingent on nothing.",
    "Covenant formula — 'I will be your God and you shall be my people': both verbs are hayah — I will hayah as your God.",
    "Prophetic fulfillment — 'the word of the LORD came (hayah) to...' — the word came into existence/happened to the prophet."
  ],
  contextNote: "The divine name YHWH is almost certainly derived from hayah. When Moses asks God's name and God says 'I AM WHO I AM,' he is giving the etymological explanation of YHWH. God simply IS — he does not derive his existence from anything. Every other thing that exists does so because God caused it to be. This is the philosophical foundation of biblical monotheism.",
  notMeantWhen: "Hayah as a regular verb is not automatically divine or theological. 'It happened that...' uses hayah as a narrative connector. Only in the Exodus 3 revelation does hayah carry the full weight of the divine name."
},

"H539": {
  primarySense: "Aman — to be firm, sure, reliable; in the causative form: to believe, to trust, to consider reliable. Faith in Hebrew is built on this word — it is reliance on something trustworthy.",
  etymology: "Related to the root that gives us 'Amen' — so be it, truly, it is firm. The Hebrew amen at the end of a prayer is the same word: 'This is firm; I stake myself on this.' Faith (emunah, H530) is derived from this root.",
  senses: [
    "To be firm or steady — pillars of a door are 'aman' (stable); a nursing mother 'aman's' a child (holds securely).",
    "To be trustworthy or reliable — a faithful servant, a reliable witness.",
    "To trust, to believe — 'Abraham believed (aman) God, and it was counted to him as righteousness' (Gen 15:6); this is the prototype of saving faith in both Testaments.",
    "Emunah (derived noun) — faithfulness; loyalty; reliability over time. God's emunah is his covenant reliability.",
    "Amen — the congregational affirmation: 'It is firm; we agree; let it be so.'"
  ],
  contextNote: "Genesis 15:6 is the hinge verse of the entire biblical theology of faith: 'He believed God, and he counted it to him as righteousness.' Paul builds his argument in Romans 4 and Galatians 3 entirely on this verse. The word aman means he considered God reliable — he staked his future on what God had said. This is what faith always is: reliance on the trustworthy character of God.",
  notMeantWhen: "Biblical faith (aman) is not intellectual assent to doctrines, nor is it a subjective feeling of confidence. It is a relational act of trust — treating God as reliable, as a firm foundation, even when circumstances suggest otherwise."
},

"H7725": {
  primarySense: "Shuv — to turn, to return; the most common word for repentance in the OT. It means a physical and moral turning around — away from sin and back toward God.",
  etymology: "Basic directional verb: to turn, to go back, to return to a place or state. Repentance in Hebrew is not primarily an emotion or a prayer; it is a change of direction.",
  senses: [
    "Physical return — returning home, returning to a country.",
    "National return from exile — shuv is the central word of the promises to return Israel from Babylon (Jer 29:10-14).",
    "Moral and spiritual return to God — 'Return (shuv) to me with all your heart' (Joel 2:12); the prophets call the nation to shuv.",
    "God's returning to his people — when Israel repents, God 'returns' (shuv) his favor: 'Return to me and I will return to you' (Zech 1:3).",
    "Restoration — 'restore (shuv) our fortunes, O LORD' (Ps 126:4)."
  ],
  contextNote: "The OT's call to repentance is always shuv — turn around, change direction, come back. It presupposes that the people have turned away and need to reorient toward God. The NT word metanoia (Greek for repentance) adds the cognitive dimension: a change of mind. Both together describe full repentance: changed thinking that produces changed direction.",
  notMeantWhen: "Shuv is not a formula, confession, or emotional experience. It is change of direction. The proof of shuv is where you end up heading. John the Baptist's 'produce fruit in keeping with repentance' (Matt 3:8) is the shuv ethic in NT form."
},

"H2403": {
  primarySense: "Chattah — sin; a missing of the mark, a falling short of what is right; the most common OT word for sin. Covers both the act and its guilt.",
  etymology: "From chata (H2398), 'to miss, to err, to stumble.' Archery language: to miss the target. Sin is primarily a failure to hit what God's standard requires — not anarchy but inaccuracy.",
  senses: [
    "Missing the mark — falling short of God's standard in action, thought, or omission.",
    "The act of sinning — a specific wrong deed (Lev 4:2-3).",
    "The guilt incurred by sin — 'bear the chattah' means to carry the legal liability.",
    "The sin offering (chattah) — the sacrifice prescribed to deal with chattah; the word covers both the problem and the solution.",
    "A life pattern of sin — cumulative wrongdoing that defines a way of life."
  ],
  contextNote: "When the Torah prescribes a 'sin offering' (chattah) it uses the same word as the problem it solves — there is a profound pun. The sin became the sacrifice; or rather, the sacrifice addressed the sin. In 2 Cor 5:21: 'God made him who knew no sin to be sin (hamartia, the Greek equivalent) for us' — the same pun operates across languages: Jesus became the chattah offering.",
  notMeantWhen: "Chattah as 'missing the mark' does not minimize sin — a missed shot can still kill. The point is that sin is a failure relative to a standard (God's character), not merely a violation of external rules."
},

"H5771": {
  primarySense: "Avon — iniquity, guilt, the state of being bent or twisted; guilt that warps the inner person and carries consequences. Heavier in moral weight than chattah.",
  etymology: "Related to avah, 'to bend, to twist.' Iniquity is what happens when sin becomes a habit and twists the inner person. It carries both the act of wrongdoing and the accumulated guilt and its consequences.",
  senses: [
    "The inner twistedness of a sinful character — not just a wrong act but a bent orientation.",
    "The guilt and punishment that wrong action carries — 'he shall bear his avon' means he will suffer the consequences.",
    "Accumulated generational guilt — 'visiting the iniquity of the fathers to the third and fourth generation' (Exod 20:5).",
    "What the Servant bears in Isaiah 53 — 'the LORD laid on him the avon of us all' (Isa 53:6); the weight of our twistedness transferred to the Suffering Servant."
  ],
  contextNote: "Avon, chattah, and pesha (H6588, transgression) are often listed together in confessions (e.g., Ps 51:1-2) to convey the total scope of moral failure: missing the mark, the inner twistedness, and the deliberate defiance. The great forgiveness declarations of the OT (Exod 34:7, Ps 32:1-2) cover all three.",
  notMeantWhen: "Avon should not be softened to 'mistake' or 'error.' The bent or twisted nature it describes is deeply ingrained moral corruption, not accidental failure."
},

"H6588": {
  primarySense: "Pesha — transgression; willful rebellion, defiant crossing of a known boundary; the most morally serious of the three main OT sin words. It implies knowing the rule and breaking it deliberately.",
  etymology: "From pasha, 'to rebel, to break away from authority, to revolt.' Used of political rebellions (1 Kgs 12:19) and moral rebellion against God. It is the sin of someone who knows better and chooses defiance.",
  senses: [
    "Political rebellion — 'Israel rebelled (pasha) against the house of David' (1 Kgs 12:19).",
    "Moral rebellion against God — conscious, willful violation of God's law.",
    "The defining sin of the wicked — not stumbling but deliberate defiance.",
    "What Isaiah 53:5 says the Servant was pierced for — 'pierced for our transgressions (pesha), crushed for our iniquities.'",
    "What forgiveness cancels — 'I, I am he who blots out your transgressions (pesha)' (Isa 43:25)."
  ],
  contextNote: "Pesha is the word that strips away excuses. It is not accident, weakness, or ignorance — it is 'I knew and I chose otherwise.' This is why the prophets use it to describe Israel's unfaithfulness: they had the covenant, they knew YHWH, and they turned to idols anyway. The cross deals with all three sin words: the missed mark, the twisted nature, and the defiant rebellion.",
  notMeantWhen: "Pesha should not be reduced to 'mistake.' It requires awareness of the standard and willful departure from it. Accidental sins (chattat bi-shegagah) used chattah vocabulary; pesha was for deliberate defiance."
},

"H1984": {
  primarySense: "Halal — to praise, to boast, to shine; the root behind Hallelu-Jah ('praise Yah!'). It means exuberant, public, boasting-in-the-good-sense celebration of God.",
  etymology: "Related to a root meaning 'to shine, to be bright, to flash.' To halal someone is to make them shine — to lift them up in words and song before others. Hallelujah = 'you all praise Yah!' (imperative plural + the short form of YHWH).",
  senses: [
    "To praise God publicly — communal worship, singing, celebrating.",
    "To boast — but in the right direction: 'Let him who boasts, boast in the LORD' (Jer 9:24).",
    "To shine or glitter — the physical brightness from which the metaphor comes.",
    "Hallel — the Psalms 113-118 called the Great Hallel, sung at Passover (the hymn Jesus sang at the Last Supper, Matt 26:30).",
    "Hallelujah — the great acclamation; appears 24 times in Psalms and four times in Revelation 19 as the eternal song of heaven."
  ],
  contextNote: "Praise in Hebrew is never passive or merely inward. Halal is public, exuberant, even noisy celebration. The Psalter ends with five consecutive psalms (146-150) each beginning and ending with Hallelu-Jah — the whole Bible crescendos into praise. Revelation 19 picks up this exact word in Greek transliteration for the heavenly anthem when Babylon falls and God reigns.",
  notMeantWhen: "Halal is not polite appreciation or quiet respect. It is declaration, celebration, boasting in the best sense — publicly honoring who God is and what he has done."
},

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  GREEK — Divine Names & Titles
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

"G2316": {
  primarySense: "Theos — God; the supreme divine being. In the NT used almost exclusively for the God of Israel, sometimes distinguished from lesser 'gods,' and applied to Jesus in key texts.",
  etymology: "Greek common noun for deity, used in the LXX to translate Elohim and El. Cognate with Latin deus. In pagan Greek religion it could refer to any member of the pantheon; the NT appropriates the word for the biblical God and redefines it by context.",
  senses: [
    "The God of Israel, Father of Jesus — the NT's primary use: 'God so loved the world' (John 3:16); 'Grace and peace from God our Father.'",
    "Jesus as Theos — John 1:1 ('the Word was God'), John 20:28 ('my Lord and my God'), Titus 2:13, 2 Pet 1:1; the NT carefully but definitely applies theos to Jesus.",
    "The Holy Spirit as divine — implied in the Trinitarian formulas (Matt 28:19; 2 Cor 13:14).",
    "Pagan deities — Acts 14:11 calls Barnabas and Paul 'gods (theoi)'; 1 Cor 8:5-6 acknowledges 'so-called gods' but asserts only one true Theos.",
    "Human authorities — Psalm 82:6 is referenced by Jesus (John 10:34): 'you are gods' — human judges bear delegated divine authority."
  ],
  contextNote: "The NT's use of theos is shaped entirely by the OT's Elohim/YHWH. When NT authors say 'God,' they mean the Creator of Genesis 1, the covenant God of Israel, the Father of Jesus Christ. The Trinitarian controversies of the early church were precisely about what it means that Jesus is also called theos — the councils determined it means full ontological equality with the Father, not a lesser divinity.",
  notMeantWhen: "Theos in a NT context does not imply polytheism. The few uses of theos for pagan deities are always contextually marked and often ironic. The NT's monotheism is strict — 'yet for us there is one God, the Father' (1 Cor 8:6)."
},

"G2962": {
  primarySense: "Kyrios — Lord, master, owner; the Greek word the LXX used to translate YHWH. When the NT calls Jesus Kyrios, it is applying the divine covenant name to him.",
  etymology: "From kyros, 'authority, power.' A kyrios is one who has the right to control, own, or direct. In everyday Greek it was used for slave-owners, masters, and as a polite honorific ('sir'). The LXX's choice of kyrios to represent YHWH loaded the word with divine content.",
  senses: [
    "Human master or owner — 'slaves, obey your earthly masters (kyrioi)' (Col 3:22).",
    "Polite address — 'sir' in conversations; the woman at the well addresses Jesus as 'kyrie' before she knows who he is.",
    "Jesus as Lord — the foundational NT confession: 'Jesus is Lord (Kyrios)' (Rom 10:9; 1 Cor 12:3); this directly applies the divine name to Jesus.",
    "YHWH in LXX quotations — when NT quotes OT passages that say 'LORD (YHWH)' and applies them to Jesus, it is explicitly equating Jesus with YHWH.",
    "The exalted name above every name — Phil 2:9-11: God gave Jesus 'the name above every name' so that every knee bows; this name is Kyrios — the divine name."
  ],
  contextNote: "Philippians 2:9-11 is decisive: it quotes Isaiah 45:23 (where YHWH says every knee will bow to HIM) and applies it to Jesus. The 'name above every name' that Jesus receives is Kyrios — which IS the divine name in the LXX. The earliest Christian confession 'Jesus is Lord' was simultaneously a theological bombshell (Jesus = YHWH) and a political statement (Caesar is not Lord).",
  notMeantWhen: "Kyrios as a polite address ('sir') carries no theological weight. The theological force comes when applied to Jesus in contexts that echo OT YHWH passages. Not every 'lord' in the NT is divine — context determines whether it is the divine name or a respectful human title."
},

"G5547": {
  primarySense: "Christos — Anointed One; the Greek translation of Hebrew Mashiach (Messiah). Applied to Jesus as the fulfillment of Israel's hope for the ultimate prophet, priest, and king.",
  etymology: "From chriō, 'to anoint with oil.' In secular Greek, a christos was simply someone smeared with oil or ointment. The LXX uses it to translate mashiach. By NT times, 'the Christ' had become a specific title for the expected deliverer.",
  senses: [
    "The Messiah of Israel — the one anointed by God to fulfill the promises to David and the prophets.",
    "Jesus' identity — 'You are the Christ, the Son of the living God' (Matt 16:16); the central confession.",
    "Used as a personal name — 'Jesus Christ' becomes almost a name by Paul's letters, though the title meaning is never lost.",
    "The scope of the anointed role — prophet (speaks God's word), priest (makes atonement), king (rules and governs).",
    "The suffering Messiah — not what first-century Jews expected; Jesus redefines the title through the cross and resurrection."
  ],
  contextNote: "In Acts 2:36, Peter says: 'God has made this Jesus, whom you crucified, both Lord and Christ.' This is the apostolic proclamation in its most compact form. 'Christ' is not Jesus' last name — it is his job title and identity. Every use of 'Christ' in Paul's letters carries the weight of the messianic office, the anointed one who fulfills all of Israel's story.",
  notMeantWhen: "Christos is not a generic word for 'savior' or 'holy man.' It is the specific title of the one Israel waited for. When it appears in the NT it always carries the full weight of OT messianic expectation — which is one reason Paul can say that in Christ all the promises of God are 'Yes and Amen' (2 Cor 1:20)."
},

"G26": {
  primarySense: "Agape — love; specifically the self-giving, other-directed love that seeks the good of another regardless of return. The NT's primary word for God's love and the love he commands.",
  etymology: "In classical Greek, agape was the weakest of four love words — not strong erotic love (eros), deep friendship (philia), or family affection (storge). The early Christians seem to have chosen precisely this neutral word and filled it with new meaning from the cross, making it the supreme theological term for love.",
  senses: [
    "God's love for the world — 'God so loved (egapēsen) the world' (John 3:16); the most famous verse in the Bible.",
    "God's love for the Son — 'the Father loves (agapa) the Son' (John 3:35); the intra-Trinitarian love.",
    "The love Christians are commanded to have — 'Love your enemies' (Matt 5:44); 'Love one another as I have loved you' (John 15:12).",
    "God's character — '1 John 4:8: 'God is agape'; love is not just something God does, it is what God IS.",
    "The supreme virtue — '1 Corinthians 13: greater than faith and hope; without it every other gift is worthless."
  ],
  contextNote: "The NT's use of agape is revolutionary. In 1 John 4:19, 'We love because he first loved us' — agape flows from God's prior action, not from our merit or the other's attractiveness. This is why the cross defines agape: 'God shows his love (agape) for us in that while we were still sinners, Christ died for us' (Rom 5:8). The definition of love is not a feeling but the cross.",
  notMeantWhen: "Agape is not a vague warm feeling or general goodwill. The NT definition requires action, sacrifice, and priority of the other's good over one's own. 'God is love' does not make God infinitely permissive — his love includes his justice and holiness."
},

"G5485": {
  primarySense: "Charis — grace; unmerited favor; gift given freely without earning. The NT's central word for God's saving action — it is the gift-quality of salvation that makes it grace and not wages.",
  etymology: "From chairō ('to rejoice, to delight in'). Charis is what gives joy — a favor, a gift, a delight. In secular Greek it meant charm or attractive quality, a gift given out of generosity, and the gratitude such a gift produces.",
  senses: [
    "God's unearned favor — 'by grace you have been saved through faith, and this is not your own doing; it is the gift of God' (Eph 2:8).",
    "The grace that defines the new covenant — 'the law came through Moses; grace and truth came through Jesus Christ' (John 1:17).",
    "A specific gift or endowment — spiritual gifts are charismata (gifts of charis); each believer receives a measure of grace for ministry.",
    "The state of being in God's favor — 'stand in this grace' (Rom 5:2).",
    "Thanksgiving — the word for 'thanks' is eucharistia (from charis); the Lord's Supper is the Eucharist, the great thanksgiving.",
    "Grace in speech and manner — 'let your conversation always be full of grace' (Col 4:6)."
  ],
  contextNote: "Paul's entire theology turns on charis. The contrast he draws throughout Romans and Galatians is charis vs. erga (grace vs. works): salvation cannot be both a gift and a wage. If grace, it is given; if wages, it is earned. The radical implication: no human merit, achievement, or law-keeping qualifies a person for salvation — only the free gift of God received through faith.",
  notMeantWhen: "Charis does not mean that God overlooks sin or that anything goes. His grace is costly — it was purchased at the cross. 'Grace' in popular usage can mean 'God doesn't care about what I do'; biblical charis is God caring so much that he gave his Son."
},

"G4102": {
  primarySense: "Pistis — faith, trust, faithfulness; the reliance on a person as trustworthy that leads to action based on that trust. In the NT, faith is always directed toward God/Christ.",
  etymology: "From peithō ('to persuade, to trust'). Pistis can mean: the act of trusting (faith), the quality of being trustworthy (faithfulness), or the body of beliefs held (the faith). Context determines which.",
  senses: [
    "Trust in God's character and promises — saving faith: 'Abraham believed God and it was counted as righteousness' (Rom 4:3, citing Gen 15:6).",
    "The faithfulness of Jesus — some scholars read pistis Christou ('faith of Christ' / 'faithfulness of Christ') as referring to Christ's own faithful obedience rather than the believer's trust in Christ.",
    "Loyalty and steadfastness — a faithful steward, a reliable friend.",
    "The body of Christian belief — 'contend for the faith (pistis) once for all delivered to the saints' (Jude 3).",
    "Miraculous faith — faith that moves mountains (Matt 17:20); a special gift of the Spirit (1 Cor 12:9)."
  ],
  contextNote: "In Hebrews 11:1, 'Faith is the substance of things hoped for, the evidence of things not seen.' This is not blind credulity — it is being so persuaded of God's reality and reliability that you act on what you cannot yet see. Every hero listed in Hebrews 11 trusted a promise whose fulfillment they did not live to see. That is the model of pistis.",
  notMeantWhen: "Pistis is not intellectual assent to doctrinal propositions. James 2:19 says the demons believe (same word) and shudder — belief without trust and obedience is not saving faith. Genuine pistis changes behavior, direction, and allegiance."
},

"G1680": {
  primarySense: "Elpis — hope; confident expectation based on reliable promise; not wishful thinking but the settled assurance of what God has said he will do.",
  etymology: "In Greek broadly means expectation (positive or negative). The NT fills it with the OT content of covenant promise — elpis becomes anticipation of what God has definitively announced through Christ.",
  senses: [
    "The hope of resurrection — 'we were saved in hope' (Rom 8:24); the bodily resurrection of Christ guarantees ours.",
    "An anchor for the soul — Heb 6:19: hope is like an anchor that reaches beyond the veil into the presence of God.",
    "The return of Christ — 'the blessed hope and the appearing of the glory of our great God and Savior, Jesus Christ' (Titus 2:13).",
    "A present orientation to the future — hope shapes how we live now: 'everyone who has this hope in him purifies himself' (1 John 3:3).",
    "Not shame — 'hope does not put us to shame' (Rom 5:5); unlike human optimism, this hope will not disappoint."
  ],
  contextNote: "The NT triad faith-hope-love (1 Cor 13:13; 1 Thess 1:3; Col 1:4-5) is a complete description of the Christian life: trust in what God has done, confident anticipation of what he will do, and active love in the present. Hope without faith has no basis; faith without hope has no horizon. Together they generate love.",
  notMeantWhen: "Biblical elpis is not optimism, positive thinking, or 'maybe things will work out.' It is confident anticipation anchored in the resurrection of Jesus — which Paul calls the first fruits guaranteeing the harvest to come (1 Cor 15:20)."
},

"G4151": {
  primarySense: "Pneuma — spirit, wind, breath; used for the Holy Spirit, the human spirit, spiritual beings, and moving air. The fundamental concept is invisible but effective power and presence.",
  etymology: "From pneō ('to blow, to breathe'). Exactly parallel to Hebrew ruach — the same range of meanings: wind, breath, the animating spirit of a person, the Spirit of God.",
  senses: [
    "The Holy Spirit (to Hagion Pneuma) — the third person of the Trinity; the promised Paraclete (John 14-16); poured out at Pentecost (Acts 2).",
    "The human spirit — the innermost person: 'the spirit of the one who knows a man's thoughts' (1 Cor 2:11).",
    "Spiritual disposition — 'a spirit of fear' / 'a spirit of gentleness' — characterizing inner orientation.",
    "Spiritual beings — angels or demons referred to as pneumata.",
    "Wind — Acts 2 Pentecost begins with 'a sound like a rushing violent wind (pneuma)'."
  ],
  contextNote: "In John 3, Jesus speaks to Nicodemus about being born 'of water and pneuma' — the same word covers the birth metaphor and the divine agent. 'The pneuma blows where it wishes' (John 3:8) uses the wind-sense to describe the Spirit's sovereign freedom. Both senses operate simultaneously, as Jesus says: 'so it is with everyone born of the Spirit.' This kind of wordplay is built into the language.",
  notMeantWhen: "Pneuma in the context of 'evil spirits' does not make those spirits divine or equal to the Holy Pneuma. The NT is explicit that all spiritual beings are creatures; only the Holy Spirit is God (Acts 5:3-4 equates lying to the Holy Spirit with lying to God)."
},

"G5590": {
  primarySense: "Psyche — soul, self, life; the living self in its totality or the animating life-principle. Like Hebrew nephesh, it refers to the whole person as a living being, not a separable immortal part.",
  etymology: "Greek psyche originally meant the animating principle of life — what makes a body alive. In Homer it is the breath-soul that departs at death. NT usage is shaped more by Hebrew nephesh than by Platonic philosophy, though overlap exists.",
  senses: [
    "The whole person, the self — 'love the Lord your God with all your psyche' (Matt 22:37) = with your whole self.",
    "Life itself — 'he who loses his psyche for my sake will find it' (Matt 16:25); physical life.",
    "The inner person — 'your soul and spirit' (Heb 4:12); 'I pray that you may prosper in all things and be in health, just as your psyche prospers' (3 John 2).",
    "The emotions, the deep self — 'my psyche is very sorrowful, even to death' (Matt 26:38).",
    "An individual person — 'three thousand psychai were added' (Acts 2:41); same usage as nephesh in census contexts."
  ],
  contextNote: "When Jesus says 'Do not fear those who kill the body but cannot kill the psyche' (Matt 10:28), he is not teaching Greek dualism (body = bad prison, soul = immortal and free). He is distinguishing levels of harm: physical death versus the death that matters more. The 'psyche' that cannot be killed by humans is not naturally immortal — it is held in God's hands.",
  notMeantWhen: "Psyche should not be read through Plato — the immortal soul naturally surviving death as its true home. The NT resurrection hope is bodily (soma pneumatikon, 1 Cor 15), not the liberation of an immaterial soul from a material body."
},

"G4561": {
  primarySense: "Sarx — flesh; the physical body or mortal human nature; in Paul especially, the mode of existence oriented by the age of Adam and inclined away from God. Context always determines which sense applies.",
  etymology: "The common Greek word for flesh/meat/body. In the LXX it translates basar. In Paul's theological usage it takes on additional connotations beyond the physical — the 'flesh' as the power of sin-inclined humanity.",
  senses: [
    "Physical body — 'the Word became flesh (sarx)' (John 1:14); literal bodily incarnation.",
    "Human nature, kinship — 'according to the flesh' meaning 'by human descent': 'Christ came from them according to the flesh' (Rom 9:5).",
    "Mortal human frailty — 'flesh and blood cannot inherit the kingdom' (1 Cor 15:50); limitation of creaturely existence.",
    "The sin-inclined self in Paul — 'I know that in my flesh nothing good dwells' (Rom 7:18); the flesh as a sphere of existence that is at war with the Spirit.",
    "External religiosity — 'we no longer know Christ according to the flesh' (2 Cor 5:16); a merely human perspective that misses the spiritual reality."
  ],
  contextNote: "In Romans 8, sarx and pneuma are contrasted as two mutually exclusive ways of living: according to the flesh (self-centered, sin-controlled, death-bound) or according to the Spirit (God-centered, Spirit-controlled, life-bound). This is not body vs. soul — physical bodies can be Spirit-filled. The 'flesh' in Paul means the whole person operating in the power of the old age, oriented toward sin and self rather than God.",
  notMeantWhen: "Sarx as flesh does not mean the physical body is evil. John 1:14 — the Word became SARX — is the clearest refutation of Gnostic body-hatred. The body is not the problem; the orientation of the self is the problem. 'Works of the flesh' in Galatians 5 include pride and division — clearly not physical sins."
},

"G3056": {
  primarySense: "Logos — word, reason, message, account; in John 1 the eternal, personal, divine Word who was with God and was God and became flesh in Jesus. The concept spans the gap between Greek philosophy and Hebrew dabar.",
  etymology: "From legō ('to speak, to say, to mean'). In Greek philosophy (especially the Stoics), logos was the rational principle ordering the cosmos. In Hebrew, dabar (the word of God) was the active, creative, powerful divine speech that accomplished what God sent it to do. John 1 fuses both.",
  senses: [
    "A word or utterance — the basic meaning: what is said.",
    "An account or reason — 'give an account (logos) for the hope within you' (1 Pet 3:15).",
    "The message proclaimed — 'the logos of the cross is foolishness' (1 Cor 1:18); the gospel message.",
    "The word of God as Scripture — 'your logos is truth' (John 17:17); 'the logos of God is living and active' (Heb 4:12).",
    "The eternal divine Son in John's prologue — 'In the beginning was the Logos, and the Logos was with God, and the Logos was God' (John 1:1); the Logos became flesh (John 1:14)."
  ],
  contextNote: "John 1:1-14 is one of the most carefully constructed passages in the NT. It deliberately echoes Genesis 1:1 ('In the beginning') and applies the wisdom-of-God traditions of Judaism to Jesus. The Logos was not created; he was 'in the beginning.' He is the agent of creation. He is the light and life of all people. And he 'pitched his tent' (eskēnōsen, echoing the tabernacle) among us — the divine presence in person.",
  notMeantWhen: "Logos in John 1 is not simply Greek philosophy's abstract rational principle. John subverts the philosophical concept by insisting the Logos is personal, relational, and became a specific human being in a specific place and time — something no Greek philosopher would have expected or accepted."
},

"G5457": {
  primarySense: "Phos — light; physical light and its metaphorical extensions: revelation, life, goodness, God's presence, truth. In John, Jesus himself is the phos of the world.",
  etymology: "Basic Greek for light. In the LXX translates Hebrew or (H216). Light in the biblical world is consistently connected with life (darkness = death), knowledge (darkness = ignorance), moral goodness (darkness = evil), and divine presence (God appeared in fire and cloud).",
  senses: [
    "Physical light — the first thing created (Gen 1:3): 'Let there be light.'",
    "God's character — 'God is light and in him there is no darkness at all' (1 John 1:5).",
    "Jesus as the light — 'I am the light of the world; whoever follows me will not walk in darkness' (John 8:12).",
    "Revelation and truth — 'your word is a lamp to my feet and a light to my path' (Ps 119:105).",
    "The calling of believers — 'you are the light of the world' (Matt 5:14); 'children of light' (Eph 5:8).",
    "Eschatological light — in the new Jerusalem there is no need of sun or moon, for God is its phos (Rev 21:23)."
  ],
  contextNote: "John's prologue introduces the Logos simultaneously as phos: 'In him was life, and the life was the light of men' (John 1:4). The darkness (skotia) tried to overcome the light and could not (John 1:5). This sets up the entire Fourth Gospel as a conflict between light (revelation, life, truth) and darkness (unbelief, death, falsehood). Jesus' 'I am the light' statements are divine claims — only God is light (1 John 1:5).",
  notMeantWhen: "Phos as physical light (the sun shining) carries no theological weight by itself. The theological sense must be established by context. 'Seeing the light' colloquially is different from the Johannine theological use."
},

"G225": {
  primarySense: "Aletheia — truth; reality as it actually is; what is genuine and reliable as opposed to false, counterfeit, or illusory. In John, truth is not primarily a proposition but a person.",
  etymology: "A-lēthēia, from the negative a- + lethē ('forgetfulness, concealment'). Truth is un-hiddenness, what is uncovered and cannot be concealed. In Greek philosophy, aletheia was the correspondence of statements with reality. The NT adds a relational and personal dimension.",
  senses: [
    "Factual truth — what actually happened, what is actually real.",
    "Moral integrity — 'speaking the truth in love' (Eph 4:15); living without deception.",
    "God's word as truth — 'Sanctify them in your truth; your word is truth' (John 17:17).",
    "Jesus as THE truth — 'I am the way, the truth, and the life' (John 14:6); truth is not merely a quality but a person.",
    "The truth that sets free — 'you will know the truth and the truth will set you free' (John 8:32).",
    "The Spirit of truth — the Holy Spirit guides into all truth (John 16:13)."
  ],
  contextNote: "Pilate's question 'What is truth?' (John 18:38) is deeply ironic in John's Gospel because he is standing in front of the one who claimed to BE the truth. The Johannine vision is that truth is not primarily a matching of words to facts but a Person who reveals reality — God himself as he actually is. This is why the Spirit's job is to 'guide into all truth': to lead believers into deeper and deeper encounter with the person who is truth.",
  notMeantWhen: "Aletheia in everyday Greek usage (and sometimes NT usage) is simply factual accuracy. The elevated theological sense (truth as person, truth as divine revelation) is specific to Johannine and some Pauline contexts."
},

"G3598": {
  primarySense: "Hodos — way, road, path; in Acts, the earliest name for the Christian movement ('the Way'). Jesus' use of 'I am the way' elevates a travel word to the central metaphor of salvation.",
  etymology: "Standard Greek for road or journey-route. Also means a way of life or manner of conduct. The LXX uses it for the 'way of the LORD' (derek YHWH), the life-path of covenant obedience.",
  senses: [
    "Physical road or path — travel narrative: 'on the way to Emmaus.'",
    "A way of life or conduct — 'teach the way of God accurately' (Acts 18:26).",
    "'The Way' — early name for the Christian movement (Acts 9:2; 19:9, 23; 24:14, 22).",
    "Jesus as the exclusive Way — 'I am the way, the truth, and the life; no one comes to the Father except through me' (John 14:6).",
    "The way that leads to life — Jesus' contrast in Matt 7:13-14: broad road to destruction, narrow hodos to life."
  ],
  contextNote: "In Acts, Christians were called 'followers of the Way' before they were called Christians. The earliest self-understanding was about following a path — the path that Jesus himself is, not merely teaches. John 14:6's 'I am the way' means Jesus doesn't point you toward the destination; he IS the path. You follow him and arrive at the Father.",
  notMeantWhen: "Hodos as a physical road carries no theological weight. The theological use is context-dependent. When Jesus says 'I am the hodos,' he is not speaking about geography but about access to the Father."
},

"G2222": {
  primarySense: "Zoe — life; particularly the kind of life that characterizes God himself, given to believers through Christ. Distinguished in John from mere biological existence (bios).",
  etymology: "Standard Greek for life. The NT contrasts it with bios (biological, physical life) by using zoe for the divine, eternal life-quality that begins now and transcends death.",
  senses: [
    "Biological life — the life of living organisms.",
    "Eternal life — not primarily quantity but quality: the life of the age to come, God's own life shared with believers.",
    "The life that Jesus IS — 'I am the resurrection and the life' (John 11:25); 'I am the way, the truth, and the life' (John 14:6).",
    "Life in the Son — '1 John 5:11-12: 'God gave us eternal life, and this life is in his Son. Whoever has the Son has zoe; whoever does not have the Son does not have zoe.'",
    "The purpose of Jesus' mission — 'I came that they may have zoe and have it abundantly' (John 10:10)."
  ],
  contextNote: "John's Gospel uses zoe 36 times — more than any other NT book. The climactic statement is 1 John 5:12: to have the Son is to have the Life. Zoe in John is not future and distant; it is present and participatory. Jesus says in John 17:3: 'This is eternal zoe: to know you, the only true God, and Jesus Christ whom you have sent.' Eternal life = knowing God, and that begins now.",
  notMeantWhen: "Zoe in NT theology is not merely living forever — it is living with a specific quality, content, and source. To have eternal life without knowing God would be an oxymoron in John's framework. Duration without relationship is not what zoe means."
},

"G979": {
  primarySense: "Bios — life; biological, physical, temporal life and the things that sustain it. Distinguished from zoe (the divine life-quality) — bios is the natural life we are born with.",
  etymology: "Greek for the life of the living organism, its duration, and the means of sustaining it (livelihood, possessions). From bios come our words biology, biography.",
  senses: [
    "Physical life, lifespan — the length of one's earthly existence.",
    "Livelihood, property — 'the widow gave all her bios' (Mark 12:44; Luke 21:4): her entire livelihood.",
    "The things of this life — 1 John 2:16: 'pride of bios' — boasting about earthly possessions and status.",
    "Earthly existence as temporary — 'whoever has worldly bios and sees his brother in need' (1 John 3:17): material goods."
  ],
  contextNote: "The distinction between bios and zoe is theologically important: Jesus came to give zoe abundantly (John 10:10), not merely to extend bios. The rich fool in Luke 12 accumulated bios but was bankrupt in zoe. The widow gave her last bios and demonstrated that she had understood something about zoe. The Sermon on the Mount repeatedly prioritizes what matters in God's kingdom over the anxious maintenance of bios.",
  notMeantWhen: "Bios is not spiritual life, eternal life, or the divine life. It is the ordinary stuff of physical existence — necessary and good, but not the highest thing."
},

"G266": {
  primarySense: "Hamartia — sin; missing the mark, falling short of God's standard. The NT's most common word for sin. Like its Hebrew equivalent chattah, the metaphor is archery: failing to hit the target.",
  etymology: "From hamartanō, 'to miss, to err, to fail.' In secular Greek it was used for aiming an arrow incorrectly, making a factual error, or missing a point. The NT fills it with the full moral weight of deliberate and unintentional wrong.",
  senses: [
    "The act of sinning — a specific wrong action: 'if your brother sins against you' (Matt 18:15).",
    "The state of sinfulness — 'all have sinned (hamartia) and fall short of the glory of God' (Rom 3:23).",
    "Sin as a power — Paul personifies hamartia as a ruling force: 'sin (hamartia) will have no dominion over you' (Rom 6:14); 'sin, seizing an opportunity, produced in me all kinds of covetousness' (Rom 7:8).",
    "Original/inherited sin-orientation — the condition that makes individual sins inevitable: 'if we say we have no hamartia, we deceive ourselves' (1 John 1:8).",
    "Jesus bearing sin — 'he made him who knew no hamartia to be hamartia for us' (2 Cor 5:21); 'the Lamb of God who takes away the hamartia of the world' (John 1:29)."
  ],
  contextNote: "Paul's personification of sin as a power (hamartia with the definite article in Romans 5-8 — literally 'The Sin') is one of the most important moves in his theology. Sin is not just what you do but a force that enslaves you, a master you serve, a law of your inner being that produces death. Liberation from sin requires not trying harder but dying to the old self and being raised in Christ (Rom 6:1-11).",
  notMeantWhen: "Hamartia in Paul's theological use is not merely moral failure or isolated bad decisions. When Paul talks about 'The Sin' (with the article) he means a structural condition of humanity in Adam — not just individual sins."
},

"G629": {
  primarySense: "Apolytrosis — redemption; the act of buying someone out of slavery or captivity by paying the required price. The NT applies this market-place and legal term to God's act of freeing humanity from sin's bondage through Christ's death.",
  etymology: "From apo- ('from, away') + lytron ('ransom price'). In the Greco-Roman world, apolytrosis referred to the purchase of a slave's freedom or the ransoming of a war captive. The price paid is integral to the word.",
  senses: [
    "Freedom purchased from slavery — the literal meaning: buying someone out of bondage.",
    "Christ's redemptive death — 'in him we have redemption through his blood, the forgiveness of sins' (Eph 1:7; Col 1:14).",
    "Present partial and future complete — 'in him you were sealed with the Holy Spirit, who is the guarantee of our inheritance until the redemption (apolytrosis) of the purchased possession' (Eph 1:13-14); present forgiveness points to eschatological completion.",
    "The redemption of our bodies — 'we groan, waiting for the apolytrosis of our bodies' (Rom 8:23); bodily resurrection is the completion of redemption.",
    "The exodus pattern — the OT prototype is YHWH redeeming Israel from Egypt with a mighty hand; the NT reads Christ's death as the greater exodus."
  ],
  contextNote: "Apolytrosis integrates with kaphar (atonement) and hilasterion (propitiation) to form the NT's theology of the cross. The cross was simultaneously: a covering of sin (kaphar), a turning away of wrath (hilasterion), a purchase of freedom (apolytrosis), and a justification (dikaiosis). Each metaphor illuminates a different facet of the same event.",
  notMeantWhen: "Apolytrosis does not require speculation about to whom the ransom was paid (debates about whether the ransom was paid to Satan or to God). The NT uses the word without specifying the recipient — the emphasis is on the price paid (Christ's blood) and the freedom achieved (from sin, death, and condemnation)."
},

"G2434": {
  primarySense: "Hilasterion — propitiation, mercy seat; the place or means by which God's righteous wrath against sin is satisfied and turned away. Paul uses this word for Jesus in Romans 3:25.",
  etymology: "From hilaskomai ('to appease, to propitiate, to make favorable'). In the LXX, hilasterion is the specific word for the mercy seat (kapporet) on the ark of the covenant — the gold lid where the high priest sprinkled blood on Yom Kippur to cover the people's sins.",
  senses: [
    "The mercy seat — the cover of the ark of the covenant; where God met Israel and accepted atonement (Exod 25:17-22; Lev 16:14-15).",
    "Jesus as hilasterion — Romans 3:25: 'God put him forward as a hilasterion by his blood, to be received by faith'; Jesus is the new mercy seat, the place where God's wrath is met with blood.",
    "Propitiation (John's use) — 1 John 2:2 and 4:10: 'he is the hilasmos for our sins'; he turned away God's wrath.",
    "The satisfaction of divine justice — the wrath of God against sin is not suppressed or overlooked but absorbed and exhausted in Christ."
  ],
  contextNote: "Romans 3:25 is one of the densest theological statements in the NT: God 'put forward' Jesus as the hilasterion — publicly displayed him as the mercy seat. On Yom Kippur, the high priest disappeared behind a veil to sprinkle blood in secret; Jesus was crucified publicly, outside the city, in full view. The new Day of Atonement is public, once-for-all, and permanently efficacious.",
  notMeantWhen: "Some translations render hilasterion as 'expiation' (removing sin) rather than 'propitiation' (turning away wrath) to avoid the language of God's wrath. The word's etymology and its LXX/Yom Kippur background supports propitiation — the satisfaction of God's justice — as the fuller meaning. Both elements are present."
},

"G40": {
  primarySense: "Hagios — holy, set apart, sacred; the Greek equivalent of Hebrew qodesh. The Holy Spirit is to Hagion Pneuma — the Set-Apart Spirit. Believers are hagioi — holy ones, saints.",
  etymology: "Related to hagnos ('pure, chaste'). Fundamentally means 'other' or 'set apart' from the common and impure. Applied to God, it means his radical otherness; applied to people and things, it means consecration to God.",
  senses: [
    "God's essential attribute — 'Holy, holy, holy is the Lord God Almighty' (Rev 4:8).",
    "The Holy Spirit — to Hagion Pneuma, the divine Spirit of the set-apart God.",
    "Believers — hagioi, 'saints' or 'holy ones' (Paul's constant address); made holy by God's call and Christ's work, not by personal achievement.",
    "Sacred objects and places — the holy temple, the holy city, holy days.",
    "The call to holiness — 'Be holy (hagioi), for I am holy' (1 Pet 1:16, quoting Lev 11:44).",
    "Sanctification — the process of being made more holy; hagiasmos."
  ],
  contextNote: "The NT uses hagioi as the standard word for Christians — they are the 'holy ones,' set apart by God. This is not a claim to moral perfection but to divine election and consecration. 'You are a royal priesthood, a holy nation' (1 Pet 2:9) — the language of Exodus 19:6 applied to the church. Holiness begins as position (declared by God) and is worked out in character (lived in the world).",
  notMeantWhen: "Hagios applied to believers is not primarily a moral evaluation of their behavior. It is a status declaration: these people belong to God. The ethical call to 'be holy' flows from this status, not the reverse."
},

"G932": {
  primarySense: "Basileia — kingdom, reign, royal rule; the active reign and realm of a king. When Jesus announces 'the kingdom of God is at hand,' he means: God is taking charge, his reign is arriving, his rule is being exercised.",
  etymology: "From basileus ('king'). Basileia is not primarily a territory but the exercise of royal authority. The kingdom of God is God's kingly rule — where God acts as king, his kingdom is present.",
  senses: [
    "God's universal and eternal sovereignty — Ps 103:19: 'His kingdom rules over all'; always true in one sense.",
    "The kingdom coming in Jesus — 'the kingdom of heaven is at hand' (Matt 4:17); the decisive arrival of God's reign in history through Jesus.",
    "The kingdom among you now — 'the kingdom of God is in your midst' (Luke 17:21); present spiritual reality.",
    "The kingdom yet to come fully — 'thy kingdom come, thy will be done on earth as in heaven' (Matt 6:10); future hope.",
    "Inheriting the kingdom — the eschatological reward for the faithful: 'Come, inherit the kingdom' (Matt 25:34).",
    "The kingdom of God vs. the kingdom of darkness — two competing reigns; Jesus' mission is the transfer of subjects from one to the other (Col 1:13)."
  ],
  contextNote: "The kingdom of God is the master theme of Jesus' preaching. Matthew uses 'kingdom of heaven' (because Jewish reverence avoided God's name); Luke and Mark say 'kingdom of God' — same reality. The kingdom is both now (Jesus' healings and exorcisms are signs of God's reign invading the old order) and not yet (it will come in fullness at the parousia). Living between already and not-yet is the defining tension of Christian existence.",
  notMeantWhen: "Basileia is not primarily about a future geographic location or the church as an institution. The kingdom is wherever God is ruling — which is wherever Jesus is. 'Entering the kingdom' is not going to heaven; it is coming under God's rule."
},

"G1391": {
  primarySense: "Doxa — glory, honor, praise, radiance; the Greek word used to translate Hebrew kavod. Applied to God it means his visible majesty; applied to humans it means honor and reputation.",
  etymology: "From dokeō ('to think, to seem, to appear'). Doxa originally meant 'opinion' or 'reputation' — what others think of you. In the LXX it was used to translate kavod, shifting its meaning toward the brilliant, weighty, visible presence of God.",
  senses: [
    "God's visible splendor — 'the doxa of the LORD settled on Mount Sinai' (Exod 24:16 LXX); blazing, overwhelming presence.",
    "The radiance of the resurrection body — 'the body is sown in dishonor, raised in doxa' (1 Cor 15:43).",
    "Human honor and reputation — 'glory from men' (John 5:44); doxa in the social sense.",
    "Doxology — ascribing glory to God: 'to him be doxa forever' (Rom 11:36).",
    "Jesus as the doxa of God — 'we have seen his doxa, doxa as of the only Son from the Father' (John 1:14); the incarnation is the kavod tabernacling among us.",
    "Shared doxa — 'the doxa that you have given me I have given them' (John 17:22); believers share the divine glory."
  ],
  contextNote: "John 17:5 is audacious: Jesus prays for 'the doxa I had with you before the world was.' Pre-existent divine glory. Then in John 17:22 he says he has given that same doxa to his disciples. The glory that was exclusively divine is to be shared with the redeemed — the destiny of humanity is participation in the life and radiance of God himself.",
  notMeantWhen: "Doxa in everyday Greek simply means reputation, fame, or opinion. The theological weight (blazing divine presence, eschatological radiance) comes from the LXX/kavod trajectory. Context determines which sense is active."
},

"G2098": {
  primarySense: "Euangelion — gospel, good news; the proclamation of what God has done in Jesus Christ: his life, death, resurrection, and lordship. Not a set of instructions but an announcement of an event.",
  etymology: "Eu- ('good') + angelion ('message, news'). In the Roman world, euangelion was the official announcement of good news from the emperor — a military victory, the birth of an heir, the beginning of a new reign. The NT hijacks this imperial vocabulary for the announcement of Jesus' reign.",
  senses: [
    "The apostolic proclamation — 'the euangelion I preached to you' (1 Cor 15:1): Christ died, was buried, was raised, appeared.",
    "The life and ministry of Jesus — Mark 1:1: 'The beginning of the euangelion of Jesus Christ, the Son of God.'",
    "The power of God for salvation — 'I am not ashamed of the euangelion, for it is the power of God for salvation to everyone who believes' (Rom 1:16).",
    "The preaching entrusted to apostles — 'I have been entrusted with the euangelion of the uncircumcised' (Gal 2:7).",
    "The content of saving faith — 'believe the euangelion' (Mark 1:15)."
  ],
  contextNote: "Paul defines the euangelion precisely in 1 Corinthians 15:1-8: Christ died for our sins, was buried, was raised on the third day, and appeared. This is the non-negotiable core — everything else is application. But the euangelion is also announcement of a new reign (basileia), which is why the political resonances with imperial propaganda matter: Caesar's 'good news' has been superseded by the real good news of the true King.",
  notMeantWhen: "Euangelion is not a synonym for the entire Bible or for any positive religious message. It has specific content: the announcement of what God did in Jesus. A 'gospel' that is merely moral advice or general spiritual uplift has lost the content that makes it good news."
},

"G165": {
  primarySense: "Aion — age, era, world-period; a span of time with distinctive characteristics. Eternal life in the NT is 'life of the age to come' — not just duration but the quality and character of the coming age.",
  etymology: "Originally meant 'lifetime, life-span.' Extended to mean 'age' as a period of time with its own character. In Jewish apocalyptic thought, history divides into 'this age' (dominated by sin and death) and 'the age to come' (God's righteous reign fully established).",
  senses: [
    "An age or epoch — 'the end of the age (aion)' (Matt 13:39, 49; 28:20); the present era coming to its close.",
    "Eternity — 'forever and ever' is literally 'into the aions of the aions' (eis tous aionas tōn aiōnōn).",
    "The world as currently constituted — 'the god of this aion has blinded the minds of unbelievers' (2 Cor 4:4); Satan's domain in the present order.",
    "Aionios (adjective) — usually translated 'eternal' but literally means 'of the age'; eternal life is the life of the coming age.",
    "The ages of God's purposes — Eph 2:7: 'in the coming aions God will show the surpassing riches of his grace.'"
  ],
  contextNote: "The Greek phrase usually translated 'eternal life' is zoe aionios — literally 'life of the age (to come).' This connects the gift directly to the Jewish concept of the messianic age. Eternal life is not just endless time in some vague sense; it is participation in the life of God's coming kingdom, which begins at the new birth. This reshapes evangelism: eternal life is not escape from earth but entry into the new creation.",
  notMeantWhen: "Aion by itself does not always mean eternal or infinite duration. 'This aion' is temporary; 'the aion to come' is unending. Context distinguishes. 'He is the same yesterday, today, and forever (eis tous aionas)' means endlessly; 'the god of this aion' means the present limited age."
},

"G166": {
  primarySense: "Aionios — eternal, age-lasting; belonging to the age to come. The adjective from aion, used in the phrase 'eternal life' (zoe aionios) to mean the kind of life that belongs to God's coming kingdom.",
  etymology: "Adjective of aion. In classical Greek it meant 'of long duration' or 'ageless.' In the NT, informed by Jewish apocalyptic, it characterizes what belongs to the eschatological age: eternal judgment, eternal life, eternal salvation.",
  senses: [
    "Eternal life — the life of the coming age; participating now in what God will make complete at the resurrection.",
    "Eternal judgment and punishment — 'these will go away into aionios punishment, but the righteous into aionios life' (Matt 25:46); the same adjective applies to both outcomes.",
    "Eternal salvation — 'he became the source of aionios salvation to all who obey him' (Heb 5:9).",
    "Eternal glory — 'an eternal weight of doxa' (2 Cor 4:17).",
    "The eternal God — 'the eternal (aionios) God' (Rom 16:26); he is not merely old but belongs to the timeless age."
  ],
  contextNote: "The debate over whether aionios means 'eternal' (infinite duration) or 'age-long' (finite but long) matters most in discussions of final punishment. The strongest argument for 'eternal' is Matt 25:46, where the same word describes both the outcome of the wicked and the outcome of the righteous. If the righteous have endless life, the wicked have endless punishment. The qualitative dimension (belonging to the age to come) does not remove the quantitative dimension (unending).",
  notMeantWhen: "Aionios does not mean 'a very long time that eventually ends' in NT usage. It describes what belongs to the ultimate, final age — which, by definition, has no successor age. Its duration is therefore unlimited."
},

"G4335": {
  primarySense: "Proseuche — prayer; communication directed to God; the whole range of address to God including petition, praise, confession, intercession, and thanksgiving.",
  etymology: "From pros- ('toward') + euchomai ('to pray, to vow, to wish'). Moving toward God with speech. Euchomai originally meant a vow or wish; proseuche is the directional, purposeful address to God.",
  senses: [
    "Personal prayer — Jesus withdrawing to pray: 'he went up on the mountain by himself to pray (proseuchesthai)' (Matt 14:23).",
    "Communal prayer — 'they devoted themselves to the apostles' teaching and fellowship, to the breaking of bread and the prayers (proseuchai)' (Acts 2:42).",
    "Intercessory prayer — praying for others: 'I urge that petitions and proseuchai be made for all people' (1 Tim 2:1).",
    "Prayer as a place — some communities had a proseuche (prayer house) where they met (Acts 16:13, 16).",
    "Continuous prayer — 'pray (proseuchomenoi) without ceasing' (1 Thess 5:17); an orientation of life toward God."
  ],
  contextNote: "Jesus modeled proseuche constantly and taught it at length (Matt 6:5-15, Luke 11:1-13). Paul's letters are saturated with it — he prays for every church and urges them to pray for him. The Lord's Prayer (Matt 6:9-13) is the pattern: address, praise, petition for God's purposes, request for needs, forgiveness, deliverance. Prayer in the NT is conversation with a Father, not performance for observers.",
  notMeantWhen: "Proseuche is not meditation on one's inner state, positive affirmation, or visualization technique. It is specifically address to a personal God who hears and acts. 'Prayer' in contexts that do not involve addressing a personal God is a different phenomenon."
},

"G1577": {
  primarySense: "Ekklesia — assembly, congregation, church; the called-out gathering of God's people. The word means 'called out' — not from the world, but called out from wherever they were to gather together.",
  etymology: "Ek- ('out of') + kaleō ('to call'). An ekklesia in Greek civic life was the assembly of citizens called together for public business. The LXX uses it for Israel's assembly (qahal) before God. The NT takes this word for the gathered community of Jesus-followers.",
  senses: [
    "A local gathered congregation — 'the ekklesia in Corinth' (1 Cor 1:2); a specific community in a specific place.",
    "All believers everywhere — 'Christ loved the ekklesia and gave himself for her' (Eph 5:25); the universal church.",
    "The gathered assembly for worship — 'when you come together as an ekklesia' (1 Cor 11:18).",
    "Israel as the assembly — Stephen refers to Israel in the wilderness as the ekklesia (Acts 7:38); continuity with OT people of God.",
    "A house church — 'the ekklesia in your house' (Philem 2; Rom 16:5)."
  ],
  contextNote: "The ekklesia is not a building, a denomination, or a religious organization — it is the gathering of people who belong to Jesus. Matthew 16:18, 'I will build my ekklesia,' is the only time Jesus uses the word in the Gospels. The ekklesia belongs to Christ and is built by him, not by human effort. Its unity is pneumatological (one Spirit) and eschatological (one people awaiting one Lord's return).",
  notMeantWhen: "Ekklesia does not mean 'called out of the world.' The root idea is 'called together' — assembled. The church is not a collection of people who have withdrawn from society but a community that gathers to be sent."
},

"G386": {
  primarySense: "Anastasis — resurrection; the standing up again of what was dead; the bodily restoration of the dead to life. The NT is insistent that resurrection is physical, not merely spiritual survival.",
  etymology: "Ana- ('up, again') + stasis ('standing, state'). To anastasis is to stand up again — a vivid word for the raising of a dead body. The Greek world mostly believed in the immortality of the soul without resurrection; the NT claim of bodily anastasis was scandalous (Acts 17:32).",
  senses: [
    "The resurrection of Jesus — 'God raised him up, loosing the pangs of death' (Acts 2:24); the historical, bodily event that is the foundation of all NT hope.",
    "The general resurrection — 'there will be a resurrection (anastasis) of both the just and the unjust' (Acts 24:15).",
    "The resurrection of believers at Christ's return — 'the dead in Christ will rise first' (1 Thess 4:16).",
    "Participation in resurrection life now — 'we were buried with him through baptism into death, so that just as Christ was raised...we too might walk in newness of life' (Rom 6:4).",
    "The first resurrection — Revelation 20:5-6 distinguishes a first resurrection (participation in Christ's reign) from the general resurrection."
  ],
  contextNote: "1 Corinthians 15 is the NT's definitive chapter on anastasis. Paul's argument: if there is no resurrection of the dead, then Christ has not been raised; if Christ has not been raised, your faith is futile and you are still in your sins. The resurrection is not a nice addition to the gospel — it IS the gospel. And Christ's resurrection is the first fruits (aparche) that guarantees the harvest of all believers' resurrections.",
  notMeantWhen: "Anastasis is not the immortality of the soul (psyche surviving without a body) nor is it 'going to heaven' in a disembodied state. It is specifically the raising of the body to transformed, glorified, incorruptible life. The NT hope is resurrection on a renewed earth, not escape to an immaterial heaven."
},

"G908": {
  primarySense: "Baptisma — baptism; immersion in water as the public, enacted declaration of death to the old life and rising to the new life in Christ. The ritual of entry into the covenant community.",
  etymology: "From baptizō ('to dip, to plunge, to immerse in water'). In secular Greek it was used for dyeing cloth (immersing so the color penetrates) and for vessels filling with water and sinking. The NT appropriates it for the water-rite of initiation.",
  senses: [
    "John's baptism — a baptism of repentance for the forgiveness of sins (Mark 1:4); preparatory, pointing to the coming one.",
    "Jesus' own baptism — the inaugural event of his ministry; the Spirit descends and the Father speaks (Matt 3:13-17).",
    "Christian baptism — in the name of the Trinity (Matt 28:19); the enacted gospel: buried (going under) and raised (coming up) with Christ (Rom 6:3-4).",
    "Spirit baptism — 'he will baptize you with the Holy Spirit and fire' (Matt 3:11); the immersion in divine presence that John's water rite signified.",
    "Suffering as baptism — Jesus asks 'Can you be baptized with the baptism I am baptized with?' (Mark 10:38); his death as a plunging under judgment."
  ],
  contextNote: "Romans 6:1-11 is the NT's deepest theology of baptism: we were baptized into Christ's death (buried with him) and raised to walk in newness of life. Baptism is not merely a symbol of something internal that already happened — it is the moment of enacted public union with Christ's death and resurrection. Paul assumes every believer has been baptized and draws ethical consequences from it.",
  notMeantWhen: "Baptisma is not a religious ceremony for infants that automatically confers salvation, nor is it a mere public symbol with no spiritual content. The NT holds together both the external act and the internal reality — what matters is that the act corresponds to a genuine union with Christ by faith."
},

"G1242": {
  primarySense: "Diatheke — covenant, testament, will; the solemn binding arrangement between God and his people. The NT draws on both meanings (covenant = binding promise; will/testament = gift given at death).",
  etymology: "From diatithemi ('to arrange, to dispose, to set in order'). In secular Greek, diatheke meant a last will and testament — the final arrangement of one's estate. The LXX used it to translate berith (covenant). The NT works with both meanings, especially in Hebrews.",
  senses: [
    "Old covenant (Mosaic) — the arrangement at Sinai, given through Moses, now obsolete and replaced (Heb 8:13).",
    "New covenant (Jeremian promise) — Jer 31:31-34 quoted at length in Heb 8:8-12; the covenant written on hearts, fully forgiving sin, enabling intimate knowledge of God.",
    "The Lord's Supper — 'This cup is the new diatheke in my blood' (Luke 22:20; 1 Cor 11:25); Jesus' death ratifies the new covenant.",
    "A will/testament — Heb 9:15-17: a diatheke only takes effect at the death of the one who made it; Christ's death activated the new covenant.",
    "The Abrahamic promise — Gal 3:15-18: God's diatheke to Abraham cannot be annulled; it predates and supersedes the law."
  ],
  contextNote: "The entire structure of the Bible (Old Testament, New Testament) is built on diatheke. The NT word for 'testament' is the same word for 'covenant.' The new covenant is new not by replacing the old God but by fulfilling what the old pointed toward: full forgiveness (not annual atonement), direct knowledge of God (not mediated through priests), and the Spirit-written law (not stone tablets). This is the climax of redemptive history.",
  notMeantWhen: "Diatheke in the NT is not a bilateral contract where both parties negotiate terms. It is always the arrangement initiated by God — the stronger party sets the terms, and the human party either accepts or rejects. The new covenant is a gift, not a negotiation."
},

"G5287": {
  primarySense: "Hypostasis — substance, reality, confidence, assurance; the stuff that underlies appearances; in Hebrews 11:1 it is the 'substance' or 'reality' of things hoped for that faith provides.",
  etymology: "From hypo- ('under') + stasis ('standing'). What stands under and supports; the underlying reality as opposed to mere appearance. The philosophical term for substance or essence. In later theology it becomes the word for the three 'persons' (hypostases) of the Trinity.",
  senses: [
    "Substance, underlying reality — what actually is, as opposed to what merely appears.",
    "Confidence, assurance — 'we have become partakers of Christ, if we hold our original hypostasis firm to the end' (Heb 3:14).",
    "The substance of faith — Heb 11:1: 'Faith is the hypostasis of things hoped for, the evidence of things not seen'; faith makes the future real and present.",
    "The nature or person of Christ — Heb 1:3: 'the exact imprint of his (God's) hypostasis'; Christ is the precise expression of God's underlying reality.",
    "The three persons of the Trinity — later theological usage: one ousia (essence), three hypostases (persons)."
  ],
  contextNote: "Hebrews 1:3 says the Son is the character (charakter) of God's hypostasis — literally the engraving-impression of God's underlying substance. As a signet ring stamps its exact image into wax, the Son stamps the exact image of the Father into history. This verse is one of the highest NT statements of Jesus' divine nature and is foundational for Nicene Christology.",
  notMeantWhen: "Hypostasis in Heb 11:1 is not merely subjective confidence (though confidence is in the semantic range). The word carries the philosophical weight of 'underlying reality' — faith gives the future a present reality, not just a hopeful feeling."
},

"G3107": {
  primarySense: "Makarios — blessed, happy, fortunate; the condition of the person who is in the favor of God and therefore truly flourishing. The Beatitudes are 'the makarioi.' Deeper than emotional happiness.",
  etymology: "In classical Greek, makariosdenoted the blessedness of the gods — untroubled, complete, needing nothing. Homer used it of the immortal gods. The LXX and NT democratize the word: the one who fears God is makarios, the poor in spirit are makarios.",
  senses: [
    "Blessed by God — 'Blessed (makarios) are the poor in spirit, for theirs is the kingdom of heaven' (Matt 5:3); the paradoxical blessedness of those who trust God in weakness.",
    "Spiritually flourishing — not merely emotionally happy but genuinely thriving in God's favor.",
    "The state of the righteous — 'Blessed is the man who walks not in the counsel of the wicked' (Ps 1:1 LXX).",
    "Eschatological blessing — 'Blessed are those invited to the wedding supper of the Lamb' (Rev 19:9).",
    "Divine blessedness — God himself is makarios: 'the blessed (makarios) God' (1 Tim 1:11; 6:15)."
  ],
  contextNote: "The Beatitudes (Matt 5:3-11) invert human expectations of blessedness: not the wealthy, powerful, and comfortable are makarios, but the poor in spirit, the mourning, the meek, the hungry. Jesus redefines flourishing entirely around relationship with God and participation in his kingdom. This is the most counter-cultural ethical statement in the ancient world.",
  notMeantWhen: "Makarios is not 'happy' in the sense of feeling good right now. The poor who mourn are makarios not because they feel fine but because they are positioned rightly before God and therefore truly flourishing at the deepest level. Temporal comfort and spiritual blessedness can be inversely related."
},

"G3875": {
  primarySense: "Parakletos — Paraclete; one called alongside to help, advocate, or comfort. Jesus' word for the Holy Spirit in John 14-16. It is also used of Jesus himself in 1 John 2:1.",
  etymology: "Para- ('alongside') + kaleō ('to call'). A parakletos is someone called to stand beside you — in a legal context, an advocate or defense attorney; in a relational context, a helper or comforter.",
  senses: [
    "The Holy Spirit as Paraclete — 'I will ask the Father, and he will give you another Parakletos, to be with you forever' (John 14:16); the Spirit continues Jesus' presence and ministry.",
    "Jesus as Paraclete — '1 John 2:1: 'If anyone sins, we have a Parakletos with the Father, Jesus Christ the righteous'; he is our advocate before God.",
    "Helper/Comforter — 'the Parakletos, the Holy Spirit, whom the Father will send in my name, will teach you all things' (John 14:26).",
    "Spirit of truth — the Parakletos 'will guide you into all truth' (John 16:13).",
    "Witness and convicter — 'when he comes, he will convict the world concerning sin, righteousness, and judgment' (John 16:8)."
  ],
  contextNote: "Jesus in John 14:16 says 'I will send you ANOTHER Parakletos' — implying that he himself was the first Parakletos during his earthly ministry. The Spirit continues everything Jesus did: teaching, guiding, advocating, empowering, convicting. The Spirit is not a replacement or lesser presence — he is the fully divine continuation of Christ's ministry in and through the church.",
  notMeantWhen: "Parakletos is not a passive 'comforter' who merely soothes feelings. The word includes the legal dimension of advocacy and the empowering dimension of assistance. The Spirit convicts, teaches, guides, and testifies — active divine ministry, not emotional support."
},

"G3306": {
  primarySense: "Meno — to remain, to abide, to dwell; to stay in a place or relationship; in John, the word for the mutual indwelling of the believer and God. 'Abide in me and I in you' (John 15:4).",
  etymology: "Standard Greek verb for remaining, staying, dwelling. The NT's common word for both physical staying and relational abiding. John uses it with extraordinary theological depth for the union between Christ and believers.",
  senses: [
    "Physical remaining — 'where are you staying (menō)?' (John 1:38); the disciples' first question to Jesus.",
    "Abiding relationship — 'abide in me and I in you' (John 15:4); the vine-branches image of organic, life-giving union.",
    "Permanent vs. temporary — 'the servant does not remain (menō) in the house forever; the son remains forever' (John 8:35).",
    "God abiding in us — '1 John 4:16: 'God is love, and whoever abides in love abides in God, and God abides in him.'",
    "The Word abiding — 'let the word of Christ dwell (menō) richly in you' (Col 3:16).",
    "Eternal permanence — 'love never fails (falls away)... these three remain (menō): faith, hope, love' (1 Cor 13:8, 13)."
  ],
  contextNote: "John 15:1-11 is the chapter of meno — Jesus uses the word 11 times. The disciples are branches; he is the vine; they must menō in him to bear fruit. Menō is not passive; it requires active trust, obedience, and prayer. The fruit it produces is love — and love is how the disciples are known as Jesus' own (John 13:35). The entire ethic of John's Gospel flows from this relational abiding.",
  notMeantWhen: "Meno in theological contexts is not merely toleration or coexistence. It is active, mutual, life-giving union. To menō in Christ means to continually draw life from him as a branch from a vine — not a static position but a living, sustained relationship."
}

}; // end window.STRONGS_SENSE_MAP
